          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "- Spines reduce water loss and protect from (12) ______"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "- Some seeds can wait for years until there is (13) ______"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "- Roots: either very deep or very (14) ______ to catch rain quickly"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "“Desert plants have developed remarkable strategies. The cactus, for example, stores water in its thick stem, while its spines — which are actually modified leaves — reduce water loss and protect it from animals. Other plants survive as seeds, which can stay dormant for years until there is enough rain. Roots are also adapted: some go extremely deep to reach underground water, while others are very shallow and spread widely, so they can catch rain the moment it falls.”"
              }
            ]
          }
